Original implementation had lots of unnecessary allocations and copying when encoding tokens to bytes. The updated code now pre-sizes the end vector of words to avoid resizing, uses references where possible, and appends encoded data directly into end vector, avoiding intermediary vector allocations.

The updated code is **~85% faster** based on (naive) benchmark, and allocates much less memory:
```rust
use ethabi::token::{LenientTokenizer, Token, Tokenizer};
use std::time::{SystemTime, UNIX_EPOCH};

fn main() {
	let input = get_input();

	let loops = 5;
	let iterations = 100000;

	let mut total_time_ns = 0;
	let mut output = vec![];

	for _ in 0..loops {
		let start_nano = SystemTime::now().duration_since(UNIX_EPOCH).unwrap().as_nanos();
		for _ in 0..iterations {
			output = ethabi::encode(&input);
		}
		let end_nano = SystemTime::now().duration_since(UNIX_EPOCH).unwrap().as_nanos();
		total_time_ns += (end_nano - start_nano)
	}

	println!("Took: {} ns/op", total_time_ns / iterations / loops);
	println!("Total: {} ns", total_time_ns);
	println!("Output size: {} bytes", output.len());
}

fn get_input() -> Vec<Token> {
	let string1 = Token::String("test".to_owned());
	let string2 = Token::String("cyborg".to_owned());
	let string3 = Token::String("night".to_owned());
	let string4 = Token::String("day".to_owned());
	let string5 = Token::String("weee".to_owned());
	let string6 = Token::String("funtests".to_owned());
	let address =
		Token::Address(LenientTokenizer::tokenize_address("25f8087EAD173b73D6e8B84329989A8eEA16CF73").unwrap().into());
	let bool = Token::Bool(true);
	let fixed_array = Token::FixedArray(vec![Token::Uint(2331.into()), Token::Uint(93523141.into()), Token::Uint(538841.into())]);
	let dynamic_array = Token::Array(vec![Token::Uint(2331.into()), Token::Uint(93523141.into()), Token::Uint(538841.into())]);
	let deep_tuple = Token::Tuple(vec![string5, string6]);
	let inner_tuple = Token::Tuple(vec![string3, string4, fixed_array, deep_tuple]);
	let outer_tuple = Token::Tuple(vec![string1, bool, string2, inner_tuple, address, dynamic_array]);

	return vec![outer_tuple];
}
```
**Before**:
```
Took: 11216 ns/op
Total: 5608343000 ns
Output size: 992 bytes
```

**After**:
```
Took: 1442 ns/op
Total: 721079000 ns
Output size: 992 bytes
```

Following returns true in spanner query.

```
SELECT TRUE OR FALSE AND FALSE
```

But current AST returns following

```go
       Expr: &ast.BinaryExpr{
          Op:   "AND",
          Left: &ast.BinaryExpr{
            Op:   "OR",
            Left: &ast.BoolLiteral{
              ValuePos: 7,
              Value:    true,
            },
            Right: &ast.BoolLiteral{
              ValuePos: 15,
              Value:    false,
            },
          },
          Right: &ast.BoolLiteral{
            ValuePos: 25,
            Value:    false,
          },
        },
      },
```

Which will evaluate to `false`, which is not correct I think and spec says AND has higher precedence.

https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/reference/standard-sql/operators

Order of Precedence | Operator | Input Data Types | Name | Operator Arity
-- | -- | -- | -- | --
11 | AND | BOOL | Logical AND | Binary
12 | OR | BOOL | Logical OR | Binary
